= Mechanism design =

== Overview ==
'''Mechanism design''' studies how rules or institutions can be constructed so that strategic participants produce desired outcomes under stated informational and incentive constraints.

Where ordinary game theory often starts with a game and analyzes behavior, mechanism design starts with an objective or allocation problem and asks what rules can induce suitable strategic behavior.

A mechanism specifies what participants can report or do, how those messages map to outcomes, and often how payments or other consequences are assigned. Incentive compatibility asks whether truthful or intended behavior is optimal under the mechanism.

Mechanism design cannot remove tradeoffs. Feasibility, efficiency, information, participation, budget balance, strategic manipulation, and distributional objectives can conflict.

== Boundaries ==
* mechanism design ≠ generic product design
* mechanism design ≠ assuming truthful behavior
* incentive compatibility ≠ fairness or optimality on every criterion

